2.1 Tracking-error state and the cascade control law
We work in the discrete-time tracking-error coordinates of the 6-DOF manipulator. Let \(\bm{q}_k,\bm{v}_k \in \mathbb{R}^6\) denote the measured joint position and velocity at sample \(k\), and let \(\bm{q}^{\mathrm{ref}}_k,\bm{v}^{\mathrm{ref}}_k\) be the time-varying joint-space reference obtained by inverse kinematics of the Cartesian end-effector target. The tracking-error state is
\[
\bm{x}_k \;\triangleq\;
\begin{bmatrix}
\bm{q}_k - \bm{q}^{\mathrm{ref}}_k \\[2pt]
\bm{v}_k - \bm{v}^{\mathrm{ref}}_k
\end{bmatrix}
\;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{12}.
\tag{1}\]
The origin \(\bm{x}_k = \bm{0}\) denotes perfect tracking. Throughout this document, \(\|\,\cdot\,\|\) denotes the Euclidean norm.
The cascade control law executed by our implementation is
\[
\bm{u}_{\mathrm{cmd},k}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\bm{u}_{\mathrm{MPC},k}}_{\text{nominal NMPC}}
\;-\;
\underbrace{\bm{d}_{\mathrm{filter},k}}_{\text{DOB estimate}}
\;-\;
\underbrace{\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}}_{\text{residual RL}},
\tag{2}\]
where all three components live in the same physical joint-torque space \(\mathbb{R}^6\). The minus sign on the RL term reflects that the policy is trained to cancel the residual disturbance, mirroring the DOB.

6.4 From the preservation criterion to a magnitude bound
Eq. 16 is geometric and depends on the unmeasurable internal quantity \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\). We now reduce it to a measurable condition on \(\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|\) alone, in three algebraic steps.
Step 1 — Square both sides. Squaring Eq. 16 and expanding the inner product on the left: \[
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\|^2
- 2\,\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\!\top}\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}
+ \|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|^2
\;\le\;
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\|^2,
\] which simplifies to \[
\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|^2
\;\le\;
2\,\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\!\top}\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}.
\tag{17}\]
Step 2 — Cauchy–Schwarz, with the known bound. The inner product on the right of Eq. 17 depends on the unmeasurable \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\). Writing \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\!\top}\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k} =
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\|\,\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|\cos\theta\) with \(\theta \in [0,\pi]\) the angle between the two vectors, the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality gives \(\cos\theta \le 1\), and the residual budget Eq. 13 gives \(\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\| \le \bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\). Together, \[
\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\!\top}\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}
\;\le\;
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\|\,\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|
\;\le\;
\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\,\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|.
\tag{18}\]
Step 3 — Solve for \(\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|\). Substituting Eq. 18 into Eq. 17, \[
\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|^2
\;\le\;
2\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\,\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|.
\] Dividing both sides by \(\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\| &gt; 0\) (the case \(\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}=\bm{0}\) is trivial) yields the ceiling.

Theorem 2 Theorem 2 (ISS action-magnitude ceiling). Suppose Assumption 1 holds and the baseline satisfies Theorem 1. For any step \(k\) at which the preservation criterion Eq. 16 is demanded, the RL action magnitude must satisfy \[
\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|
\;\le\;
2\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}
\;=\;
\frac{2\,c_x\,r^2}{\gamma_0}.
\tag{19}\]

6.5 The clipping law
The ceiling Eq. 19 is enforced as a stateless, radial saturation layer interposed between the actor network and the joint command. If the raw output already satisfies the ceiling, it passes through unchanged; otherwise it is scaled radially back onto the boundary of the admissible ball:

Definition 2 Definition 2 (ISS clipping law). The clipped RL torque applied to the cascade is \[
\boxed{\quad
\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}^{\mathrm{clip}}
\;=\;
\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\cdot
\min\!\Bigl[\,1,\;
\frac{2\,c_x\,r^2}{\gamma_0\,\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\|}
\Bigr].
\quad}
\tag{20}\]

Two properties of Eq. 20 deserve emphasis:

Direction-preserving. The scaling is radial, so a well-aimed but over-strong correction is softened, never redirected. This is important because the policy’s reward function rewards alignment of \(\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL}}\) with \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\); per-component clamping would destroy that alignment.
Stateless. The clip depends only on the constants \(c_x,\gamma_0,r\) (fixed once and for all) and on the current \(\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\). It does not depend on the state \(\bm{x}_k\) or on the residual \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\). This makes the clip cheap on hardware and trivial to implement.

6.6 Geometric interpretation and honest caveat
Eq. 19 is a necessary condition for Eq. 16 to be satisfiable; it is not a blanket sufficient condition. Reading Eq. 17 exactly, the preservation criterion holds if and only if \(\|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}\| \le 2\,\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\|\,\cos\theta\). The largest value the right-hand side can take, over all admissible residuals and all angles, is \(2\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\), attained only when the action is perfectly aligned with the residual at the worst-case magnitude. Thus \(2\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\) is the outer envelope: any action exceeding it cannot satisfy Eq. 16 under any alignment, and is therefore provably unsafe; the clip removes exactly these actions.
Inside the envelope, alignment is the policy’s job, not the clip’s. What the clip guarantees, even in the adversarial worst case where the clipped action is anti-aligned with the residual, is a hard upper bound on the effective residual via the triangle inequality: \[
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{eff},k}\|
\;\le\;
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\| + \|\bm{u}_{\mathrm{RL},k}^{\mathrm{clip}}\|
\;\le\;
\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}} + 2\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}
\;=\;
3\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}.
\] Plugging \(3\,\bar{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\) back into the ISS inequality Eq. 12 in place of \(\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res}}\|\) shows that the closed loop remains ISS with an enlarged—but still finite—ultimate radius \(\sqrt{3}\,r\). The cage never opens.






TipWhat the clip guarantees, in one sentence



If the RL policy aligns with the residual (its trained behaviour), the error settles inside the target radius \(r\); if the policy fails arbitrarily, the error stays inside \(\sqrt{3}\,r\).

7.1 Convergence coefficient \(c_x\)
By Assumption (A2), \(c_x\) is the largest scalar satisfying \(\ell(\bm{x},\kappa_N(\bm{x})) \ge c_x \|\bm{x}\|^2\). Equivalently, by Lemma 1, \(c_x\) is the largest scalar consistent with the descent property \[
\frac{V_N^*(\bm{x}_k) - V_N^*(\bm{x}_{k+1}^{\mathrm{nom}})}
     {\|\bm{x}_k\|^2}
\;\ge\; c_x
\quad\text{for all nominal } \bm{x}_k\in\mathcal{X}.
\tag{21}\]
A nominal trajectory is one in which the disturbance is small or absent; on real hardware we approximate “nominal” by quiet operating conditions (no intentional disturbance, no external excitation, no payload changes). The empirical estimator is then

Definition 3 Definition 3 (Empirical convergence coefficient). Let \(\{(\bm{x}_k, V_N^*(\bm{x}_k))\}_{k=1}^{K}\) be a sequence of state–cost pairs logged from quiet closed-loop operation. The empirical convergence coefficient is the lower 5th percentile, \[
\hat{c}_x \;=\;
\mathrm{percentile}_{5}\!\Biggl\{\;
\frac{V_N^*(\bm{x}_k) - V_N^*(\bm{x}_{k+1})}
     {\|\bm{x}_k\|^2}
\;:\;
\|\bm{x}_k\| &gt; \epsilon,\;\;
V_N^*(\bm{x}_k) &gt; V_N^*(\bm{x}_{k+1})
\;\Biggr\},
\tag{22}\] where \(\epsilon\) is a small floor (e.g. \(10^{-3}\,\)m) preventing the ratio from exploding near the origin.

The use of the 5th percentile rather than the strict minimum trades off two competing concerns: the strict minimum is contaminated by isolated numerical outliers (e.g. MPC solves that hit the iteration cap), while the median over-estimates the descent and would violate the lower-bound interpretation for a non-trivial fraction of samples. The 5th percentile gives a robust lower envelope that is safe in the ISS sense (i.e. a valid lower bound for \(\ge 95\%\) of samples).
This procedure can be applied identically in simulation and on real hardware. We recommend taking \(\hat{c}_x = \min(\hat{c}_x^{\mathrm{sim}},\hat{c}_x^{\mathrm{real}})\) to absorb the discrepancy caused by unmodelled friction on the physical plant.

7.2 Disturbance gain \(\gamma_0\)
By Assumption (A3) and the construction of Eq. 11, \(\gamma_0\) is the smallest scalar that upper-bounds the cost change induced by a state displacement of the magnitude of \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\). Re-arranging the ISS inequality Eq. 12, \[
\gamma_0 \;\ge\;
\frac{-\bigl[V_N^*(\bm{x}_{k+1}) - V_N^*(\bm{x}_k)\bigr]
       \;+\; c_x\,\|\bm{x}_k\|^2}
     {\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\|}
\quad\text{whenever } \|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\| &gt; 0.
\tag{23}\]
The numerator is computable from logged \(V_N^*\) and \(\|\bm{x}_k\|\); the denominator requires the clean ground-truth residual. This is the critical practical asymmetry:

In simulation, \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}\) is computable exactly from \(\bm{d}_{\mathrm{true},k}^{\mathrm{sim}} -
\bm{d}_{\mathrm{filter},k}\), where the “true” disturbance is obtained by inverse dynamics with the ground-truth joint acceleration provided by the physics engine.
On real hardware, the joint acceleration is reconstructed by finite differences, which injects numerical noise into the residual estimate. The empirical fit Eq. 23 would under-estimate \(\gamma_0\) (because the noisy denominator is inflated), which is the unsafe direction.

The remedy is to estimate \(\gamma_0\) in simulation only, then transfer the value to real hardware. The transfer is exact because \(\gamma_0\) is a property of the cost function \(V_N^*\), and \(V_N^*\) is the same mathematical function in sim and real (same NMPC weights, same URDF, same horizon, same sample time).

Definition 4 Definition 4 (Empirical disturbance gain). Let \(\{(\bm{x}_k, V_N^*(\bm{x}_k), \bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\mathrm{sim}})\}_{k=1}^{K}\) be logged from a baseline simulation run with the full disturbance curriculum active. The empirical disturbance gain is the 95th percentile, \[
\hat{\gamma}_0 \;=\;
\mathrm{percentile}_{95}\!\Biggl\{\;
\frac{-\Delta V_k + \hat{c}_x\,\|\bm{x}_k\|^2}
     {\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\mathrm{sim}}\|}
\;:\;
\|\bm{d}_{\mathrm{res},k}^{\mathrm{sim}}\| &gt; \delta,
\;\;\text{(ratio)} &gt; 0
\;\Biggr\},
\] where \(\Delta V_k = V_N^*(\bm{x}_{k+1}) - V_N^*(\bm{x}_k)\) and \(\delta\) is a small floor preventing division by near-zero residuals.

The 95th percentile—rather than the strict maximum—gives a robust upper envelope in the same way the 5th percentile gave a robust lower envelope for \(\hat{c}_x\).

